INT. CLOCKMAKER'S SHOP - NIGHT

Ticking, everywhere. Cuckoos, grandfathers, carriage clocks. A workbench lit by a green lamp. The window shows an empty street slick with rain.

INSPECTOR BARNABAS HOLLOWAY (50s, trench coat) stands over a body: the CLOCKMAKER (70s, apron, eyes open) lying beside the bench.

A young constable, ROSALIND PEEL (20s, notebook), hovers near the door.

A woman in a pearl-grey raincoat steps in, shaking her umbrella. A STRANGER (30s, calm eyes).

STRANGER
Inspector.

HOLLOWAY
Who let you in?

STRANGER
The door was open.

ROSALIND
It wasn't, sir.

HOLLOWAY
Name.

STRANGER
Winifred.

HOLLOWAY
Winifred what?

STRANGER
Winifred Arkwright. His niece.

ROSALIND
He had no niece.

WINIFRED
He had me.

Holloway crouches by the body. The clocks tick on. Rosalind scribbles.

HOLLOWAY
Time of death?

ROSALIND
Eleven-twelve, sir. The big clock stopped.

HOLLOWAY
Stopped?

WINIFRED
Someone stopped it.

HOLLOWAY
You'd know, would you?

WINIFRED
I would.

Holloway looks up. The woman is smiling faintly. Rosalind frowns, then stares at the grandfather clock. Its hands read eleven-twelve. Its pendulum swings.

ROSALIND
Sir. It's running.

HOLLOWAY
Then it was reset.

WINIFRED
By me.

A silence. Rosalind's hand drifts to her whistle. Holloway rises slowly.

HOLLOWAY
Miss Arkwright.

WINIFRED
Inspector.

HOLLOWAY
You wouldn't be telling me you did this.

WINIFRED
No. I'm telling you what he told me.

HOLLOWAY
Who?

WINIFRED
The clocks.

Every clock in the room chimes at once. Rosalind jumps. Holloway doesn't.

FADE OUT.
